{#
  /menu/. REBUILT AS A CHOOSER 2026-09-15, ported from
  _reference/menu-concepts/tajima-menu_mockup-1_location-first.html.

  ============================ WHY THIS PAGE CHANGED ============================

  It was a brand-wide dish list and location pages were a filtered view of it.
  The premise does not hold. Counted from the rooms' own Toast catalogs, TEN
  dishes are served at all six San Diego rooms and another ten at five, every
  one of those ten missing only Plaza Bonita. Mercury carries 56 against Crown
  Point's 24 and is a substantially different restaurant rather than a location
  with extras.

  So a shared menu is eight dishes, which is a highlight reel and not a menu
  page. The menu moved to the room. This page's job is getting a reader to the
  right room, and showing enough food on the way that a brand-level search
  lands on something.

  ============================ /menu/ AGAINST /locations/ ============================

  They overlap unless the jobs are separated, and they are:

    /locations/  WHERE CAN I EAT. Addresses, hours, phone numbers, directions,
                 parking. A reader who knows what they want and needs to know
                 where to get it.

    /menu/       WHAT DO THEY SERVE. Sorted by menu difference: what each room
                 has that the others do not, and how many dishes that is. A
                 reader who has not chosen a room yet and will choose on food.

  Neither page repeats the other's columns. This page prints no address, no
  hours and no phone number, and its cards link to the room rather than the map.

  ============================ WHAT IS NOT PORTED ============================

  The mockup is a single-page app: it swaps a chooser view for a menu view in
  the browser and renders both from one JS object. This site is static Eleventy
  and the rooms already have real URLs. So the cards are links, and the view
  switcher, the pill bar, the lightbox and the sheet are dropped.

  CONNOR'S CARD COPY IS ALSO NOT PORTED, and that is a fact check rather than a
  stylistic preference. His blurbs put Crown Point in "Pacific Beach", which
  contradicts locations.json (locality San Diego, neighborhood Crown Point;
  Pacific Beach appears only inside a Toast routing slug this repo already flags
  as not NAP), give East Village as "9th and E" against its 901 E Street, and
  call Convoy "Late night", which nothing confirms. The cards are built from
  locations.json's own `summary` and `neighborhood`, which are confirmed and
  already in the brand voice.

  THE TAGS ARE COMPUTED, NOT AUTHORED. See components/menu-chooser-card.njk.
#}

{%- set spriteIcons = ["ramen-bowl", "chopsticks-noodles"] %}
{% include "components/icon-sprite.njk" %}

{%- set sanDiego = locations.items | where("region", "san-diego") %}
{%- set sdRoomIds = [] %}
{%- for room in sanDiego %}{% set sdRoomIds = (sdRoomIds.push(room.id), sdRoomIds) %}{% endfor %}
{%- set hawaii = locations.items | where("region", "hawaii") %}

{#- src/_data/coreMenu.js computes every dish list this page uses, never by
    hand, from the SAME roomMenus rows the location pages render, so this
    page and a room's own menu cannot disagree about what it serves. `core`
    itself (five-of-six and up) is no longer read directly here; only
    `universalFeaturable`, below. See that file for the threshold and the
    full breakdown. -#}
{#- BENTO DISHES: served at all six San Diego rooms (coreMenu.universal),
    minus the standing do-not-feature rows (coreMenu.universalFeaturable).
    Carnitas Ramen is the case that matters here, added 2026-09-19: it
    genuinely qualifies as universal, and menu.json's featurable flag
    (Open Decision #4, "listed plainly everywhere, never a photo, card,
    hero or callout") is exactly why a bento tile is the wrong place for
    it regardless. `count === sanDiego.length` still guarantees no
    missingNote can reach this list; `featurable !== false` guarantees no
    do-not-feature row can either. Both conditions live in coreMenu.js, not
    here, so this section reads as a straight loop over whatever the data
    layer already decided is safe to show. -#}
{%- set bentoDishes = coreMenu.universalFeaturable %}

{# ==================== MASTHEAD ==================== #}
<section class="mchoose-mast" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <p class="mchoose-mast__kicker">{{ site.name }}, San Diego</p>
    <h1 class="mchoose-mast__title" id="page-title">Six rooms, <em>six menus.</em></h1>
    <p class="mchoose-mast__note">
      The bowls below are on every San Diego menu. Everything around them changes room to room.
      Pick where you are eating.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ==================== THE BENTO GRID · universal, featurable dishes ====================
   RESTORED 2026-09-18, NARROWED AGAIN 2026-09-19. The grid this page shipped
   with is the one that broke: one shared layout stretched across six rooms
   with barely-overlapping menus, which is exactly the failure the
   2026-09-15 rewrite documents at the top of this file. It is not rebuilt
   from scratch here, because the failure was never the grid mechanics,
   `.bento`/`.dish` in css/pages/menu.css (git history: bd3059e^, restored
   intact) draws a card the same way it always did. The failure was scope:
   a bento cell has no room to print "not served at Plaza Bonita", so
   nothing that carries an exception belongs in one, and no room to print a
   client's do-not-feature rule either, so nothing under that rule belongs
   here regardless of how many rooms serve it. `coreMenu.universalFeaturable`
   is built to guarantee both: see that file. Photo and name only, one line,
   since /menu/ has printed no prices since Open Decision #24 resolved that
   at client review.

   THE COUNT IS PRINTED, NEVER HARD-CODED, on purpose: it was eight until
   Carnitas Ramen's featurable:false rule pulled it to seven, and the count
   in the heading and the note below both read `bentoDishes | length` so a
   future change to either list (a room adding or dropping a dish, a
   different do-not-feature call) updates the sentence along with the grid
   rather than leaving a stale number for someone to catch later.

   Deliberately NOT an attempt at the old hand-placed asymmetric layout
   (.dish--ramen-tonkotsu and its seven siblings): that layout was hard-coded
   to eight specific ramen names from the pre-2026-09-15 menu, none of which
   match today's list (which mixes ramen with Karaage and Pork Gyoza,
   izakaya dishes the old layout had no slots for at all), and hand-coding
   dish names into placement CSS is the same fragility this whole pass
   exists to remove. Even columns instead: four across, no cell reads as
   more equal than another, an uneven last row is fine, and the grid does
   not go stale the day the menu or the feature list does. #}
{#- id="signature" IS LOAD-BEARING. The homepage bowl tiles and both Noodle
    Room links point here rather than at the top of the page: they promise food,
    and the top of this page is a masthead. -#}
<section class="sect sect--rule" id="signature" aria-labelledby="signature-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <header class="sect__head">
      <h2 class="sect__title" id="signature-title">The {{ bentoDishes | length }} <em>everyone makes.</em></h2>
      <p class="sect__note">
        No exceptions: every one of these is on all six San Diego menus. Nothing below is missing
        from a room, which is what makes it safe to show without naming one.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ul class="bento bento--signature">
      {%- for dish in bentoDishes %}
      <li class="dish">
        {%- if dish.photo %}
        <span class="dish__photo">
          <img src="{{ dish.photo.card | default(dish.photo.src) }}" alt="{{ dish.photo.alt }}" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            width="{{ dish.photo.cardWidth | default(dish.photo.width) }}" height="{{ dish.photo.cardHeight | default(dish.photo.height) }}">
        </span>
        {%- else %}
        <span class="dish__photo dish__photo--none" aria-hidden="true"><svg><use href="#i-ramen-bowl"></use></svg></span>
        {%- endif %}
        <span class="dish__body">
          <span class="dish__name">{{ dish.name }}</span>
        </span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ==================== THE CHOOSER ====================
   The mockup's card grid: three across, hairline gaps on a rule-coloured
   ground, two-pixel ink rules top and bottom, cards that fill with ink on
   hover. Each one is a link to that room, not a view swap.

   THE TRANSITION IS WRITTEN ON PURPOSE, 2026-09-18. The bento section above
   is the highlight reel; this is not a smaller, lesser version of the same
   idea, it is the other half of the page's own thesis (what do they serve,
   sorted by menu difference), so the copy says so rather than letting the
   grid just follow the bento with no bridge. #}
<section class="sect" aria-labelledby="chooser-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <header class="sect__head">
      <h2 class="sect__title" id="chooser-title">What each room <em>adds.</em></h2>
      <p class="sect__note">
        Past the {{ bentoDishes | length }} above, every room's menu is its own. Mercury runs a full
        sushi program the others do not; Plaza Bonita runs a quick-serve counter. Pick a room to see
        what it serves beyond the shared list.
        Addresses, hours and directions are on
        <a class="sect__link" href="/locations/">the locations page</a>; this one sorts by what
        comes out of the kitchen.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mchoose-grid">
      {%- for loc in sanDiego %}{% include "components/menu-chooser-card.njk" %}{% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ==================== MAUI ====================
   Set apart, and the separation is the point rather than a layout preference.
   Connor's sheet has no Maui column, so its dish count comes from a different
   source on a different date, and SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md forbids ranking it among
   the San Diego rooms in any case. #}
<section class="sect sect--alt sect--rule" aria-labelledby="maui-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <header class="sect__head">
      <h2 class="sect__title" id="maui-title">And one in <em>Kihei.</em></h2>
      <p class="sect__note">
        Maui runs a menu of its own. {{ coreMenu.mauiOnly | length }} of its dishes are on no San
        Diego menu at all, which is why it sits here rather than ranked with the six.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mchoose-grid mchoose-grid--single">
      {%- for loc in hawaii %}{% include "components/menu-chooser-card.njk" %}{% endfor %}
    </div>

    {#- WHAT ONLY MAUI SERVES, listed rather than summarised, because "its own
        menu" is a claim and this is the evidence for it. Computed in
        coreMenu.js from the same rows Maui's own page renders.

        The four dishes that share a San Diego NAME and are a different
        preparation are deliberately not in this list: saying Maui is the only
        room with a California Roll would be false, and saying nothing about it
        is honest. src/_data/menuAliases.json carries those four with both
        descriptions. -#}
    <p class="mchoose-only">
      <span class="mchoose-only__label">Only in Kihei</span>
      {{ coreMenu.mauiOnlyNames }}.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

